# WHAT EACH RULE ACTUALLY EXAMINED, AND WHETHER THAT IS ENOUGH TO MEAN ANYTHING. One row per
# rule: how many items it looked at, how many breached it, and a verdict on the count itself.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ A RULE THAT EXAMINED NOTHING IS NOT A RULE THAT PASSED. This repository names the trap
#    in one sentence -- "a bound with nothing to bound passes loudest" -- and this is where it
#    is answered rather than restated. An empty violations table beside a zero here is a
#    checker reporting on its own silence.
#      examined = 0  ⛔ VACUOUS. The rule proves nothing whatever.
#      examined < 3  ⚠️  thin. One or two rows is a demonstration, not evidence.
#      otherwise     ok.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND A THIN VERDICT IS USUALLY A FACT ABOUT AUTHORSHIP, NOT ABOUT THE WORLD. If a rule
#    examines one row, the question is who wrote the corpus and what they filed -- not whether
#    the model is wrong. A FIXTURE can raise the number honestly, because a fixture is a
#    stipulation; a corpus filing cannot, because that would mean inventing an observation
#    nobody made.
#
# ⛔⛔ THE VACUOUS ROW IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS IS A `GROUP BY` OVER A ROSTER JOIN RATHER THAN
#    OVER A POPULATION. Group a filtered population and a rule with nothing to examine
#    produces no row at all -- so the checker's most important verdict would be delivered by
#    silence, which is indistinguishable from the rule not existing. Every check emits its
#    roster row first, so `examined = 0` is a row you can read.
#
# ⛔ EVERY COUNT HERE IS OVER THE SAME RELATION THE VIOLATIONS ARE FILTERED FROM. Nothing
#   restates a population, so nothing here can be about a different set of rows than the rule
#   it reports on.
-- checks/all.sqlc, grouped by rule.
SELECT c.rule,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE c.violates IS NOT NULL) AS examined,
       count(*) FILTER (WHERE c.violates)             AS violations,
       CASE WHEN count(*) FILTER (WHERE c.violates IS NOT NULL) = 0
                 THEN '⛔ VACUOUS - proves nothing'
            WHEN count(*) FILTER (WHERE c.violates IS NOT NULL) < 3
                 THEN '⚠️  thin'
            ELSE 'ok' END                             AS verdict
FROM (
    :compose(checks/all.sqlc)
) c
GROUP BY c.rule
ORDER BY examined DESC, rule
